WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.200
Keep an eye out for sandworms, because it's&nbsp;
time to delve into the hidden history of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.720
of sci-fi's most popular franchises. From the&nbsp;
classic book series to the movie adaptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.720
this is the untold truth of "Dune."

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.320
Between the coastal town of Florence, Oregon,&nbsp;
and the Pacific Ocean are the Oregon Dunes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.920
massive sand hills that, in the 1950s, the U.S.&nbsp;
Department of Agriculture discovered were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.480
moving. They attempted to use certain breeds&nbsp;
of grasses to stabilize and calm the dunes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.920
before they could continue to grow, move, and,&nbsp;
as Frank Herbert wrote to his literary agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.920
"swallow whole cities,&nbsp;
lakes, rivers, and highways.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.000
Having published a few successful science&nbsp;
fiction novels already, Herbert traveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.040
to Florence in 1957 to explore this bizarre&nbsp;
scientific phenomenon and to write an article.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.200
That piece was never completed, but the&nbsp;
concepts of precious natural resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.680
monstrous sand dunes, and the mysteries&nbsp;
that may lay within led to "Dune."

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.440
After five years of research&nbsp;
and writing, Herbert published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.760
"Dune World" and "The Prophet of Dune" in serial&nbsp;
form in the Analog magazine from 1963 to 1965.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.120
The author then compiled it into a single-volume&nbsp;
novel, "Dune," which was published by technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:17.760
book publisher Chilton Books in August 1965&nbsp;
after more than 20 other publishers rejected it.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.400
"Dune" was published in 1965, but it&nbsp;
didn't hit the big screen until 1984.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:31.120
For almost that entire span of 20 years, efforts&nbsp;
were underway to adapt the book. In 1971,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.440
Apjac International, best known for the "Planet&nbsp;
of the Apes" movies, acquired the movie rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.240
and offered the director's chair to David Lean,&nbsp;
the legendary British filmmaker behind another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.120
desert-based movie, "Lawrence of Arabia." Lean&nbsp;
passed, as did British director Charles Jarrott.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.200
While a director was sought out, screenwriters&nbsp;
worked on the script and production plans were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.800
put into place, but it all fell apart when&nbsp;
Apjac head Arthur P. Jacobs died in 1973.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.920
Apjac sold the rights to "Dune"&nbsp;
to a group of French investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.080
who hired avant-garde film and&nbsp;
theatrical director Alejandro Jodorwsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.000
as director. While he'd only directed&nbsp;
three full-length films at that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.360
he definitely had the enthusiasm and ambition&nbsp;
necessary to direct "Dune" ... and then some.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.240
As detailed in the fascinating 2013 documentary&nbsp;
"Jodorowsky's Dune," the director planned to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.880
a 14-hour movie with a dream cast including&nbsp;
Orson Welles, Mick Jagger, and Salvador Dali,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.760
with a soundtrack provided by Pink Floyd. To&nbsp;
nail down the production design, Jodorowsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.440
consulted such innovative artists as Moebius and&nbsp;
H.R. Giger. He also wanted to depict melange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:39.120
"the spice," as a magical blue sponge and&nbsp;
completely change the ending of the novel.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.720
When that project died, the rights were&nbsp;
sold to producer Dino De Laurentiis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.640
who hired Ridley Scott to direct. He dropped&nbsp;
out because, as he related in a 1999 biography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.040
he was ready to film the movie immediately,&nbsp;
but it was going to take at least two and half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.560
more years of pre-production. Ultimately, the&nbsp;
gig went to then-cult movie icon David Lynch.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.280
"What the hell?"

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.680
"Dune" finally started being&nbsp;
committed to film in 1983&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.640
at Churubusco Studios in Mexico City. That&nbsp;
location was chosen in part because it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.120
adjacent to large swaths of desert that&nbsp;
could stand in for the planet Arrakis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.680
and because the Mexican economy was such that&nbsp;
it would cost Universal Pictures far less to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.960
shoot the movie than it would have cost in the&nbsp;
U.S. Shooting in L.A., the producer estimates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.040
could have bumped the "Dune" price tag to $75&nbsp;
million; it ultimately cost about $40 million.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.880
And filmmakers made sure that every penny&nbsp;
counted. To create the intricate worlds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.680
Frank Herbert's novel, one of the biggest&nbsp;
film crews of all time was assembled:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:46.000
900 workers toiled for months to build&nbsp;
70 sets on eight sound stages. More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.160
than 200 people alone had to clean three square&nbsp;
miles of desert — down on their hands and knees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.920
no less — of scorpions, snakes, and cactuses&nbsp;
to replicate the lifeless surface of Arrakis.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.800
Universal hoped "Dune" would be the next "Star&nbsp;
Wars," but while it was certainly as imaginative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:11.840
and ambitious as George Lucas's epic, it ended up&nbsp;
being a huge disaster. Critics absolutely hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:19.680
it: Roger Ebert said "Dune" was, quote, "a real&nbsp;
mess, an incomprehensible, ugly, unstructured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.880
pointless excursion into the murkier realms of one&nbsp;
of the most confusing screenplays of all time."

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.640
David Lynch's "Dune" lacked the broad appeal and&nbsp;
fun of more popular sci-fi like "Star Wars" — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.520
Universal knew it on some level before the movie&nbsp;
was released, distributing hundreds of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.480
of vocabulary guides to movie theaters in order&nbsp;
to familiarize audiences with the dense world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:46.240
of "Dune" settings, characters, and words. The&nbsp;
guides didn't help much—it ultimately earned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.800
just $30 million at the box office, not&nbsp;
even clearing its $40 million budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.840
As a result, Frank Herbert's other "Dune"&nbsp;
books were never adapted for the big screen.​

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.800
"Dune" and its sequels make for a thoughtful,&nbsp;
sprawling, imaginative sci-fi epic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.600
and the books were popular in the '60s&nbsp;
and '70s, which can mean only one thing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:12.320
It inspired a lot of progressive rock. In&nbsp;
1977, jazz keyboardist David Matthews released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.000
"Dune," inspired by the books. In 1979,&nbsp;
experimental German electronic music composer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.600
and Tangerine Dream member, Klaus Schulze released&nbsp;
"Dune," an album of the music inspired by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.560
novel. That same year, French electronica&nbsp;
pioneer Bernard Szajner, under the name Z,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.520
released a "Dune"-themed album called "Visions&nbsp;
of Dune." Around the time of the film's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:39.040
Iron Maiden included the song "To Tame a Land" on&nbsp;
its 1983 album "Piece of Mind," having changed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.480
song from its original title of "Dune," because&nbsp;
Herbert wouldn't give permission. In 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.960
German metal band Golem released the&nbsp;
"Dune"-themed concept album "The 2nd Moon."

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.520
Lynch estimated that his shooting&nbsp;
script would translate to a running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.560
time of about three hours, but once it was&nbsp;
filmed, edited, and effects were added in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.880
"Dune" wound up being well over four. Seeing that&nbsp;
as completely unmarketable, Universal ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.000
Lynch to cut a bunch of scenes and streamline&nbsp;
the plot; because of all that unused footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.280
rumors have persisted for more than 30 years that&nbsp;
there's a special, Lynch-curated "director's cut."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.600
There isn't — Universal has approached&nbsp;
Lynch, but his displeasure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.680
disappointment in the film has kept him from&nbsp;
returning to cobble together another version.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:27.360
There is, however, a three-hour version&nbsp;
of "Dune" with extra scenes added in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.160
assembled without Lynch's involvement for&nbsp;
television broadcast. Lynch was so upset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.240
that he demanded his name taken off the&nbsp;
credits for this version, replaced with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.120
the standard director pseudonym of Alan Smithee,&nbsp;
with the fake name "Judas Booth" subbed in for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.080
screenwriter — so named because Universal&nbsp;
betrayed Lynch, like Judas did to Jesus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.840
and also killed the movie, like John&nbsp;
Wilkes Booth did to Abraham Lincoln.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.280
In a career filled with weird, challenging&nbsp;
movies and zero bona fide box office hits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.720
"Dune" is about the only project&nbsp;
that writer-director David Lynch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.520
says he truly wishes he hadn't made. In&nbsp;
fact, he says it even soiled him as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.160
artist. Lynch later told Extrovert that&nbsp;
he only has himself to blame. He said,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.960
"I probably shouldn't have done that picture, but&nbsp;
I saw tons and tons of possibilities for things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.640
I loved, and this was the structure to do them&nbsp;
in. There was so much room to create a world."

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:25.600
"Far off in the control rooms of&nbsp;
spice gas, traveling… without moving."

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.400
After years of research and writing, Frank Herbert&nbsp;
completed two short science fiction novels — "Dune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:39.520
World" in 1963, and "The Prophet of Dune" in 1965.&nbsp;
The stories were published in serialized form in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.840
Analog Magazine, one of the major outlets&nbsp;
for genre stories in the mid-20th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.640
At the time, short stories dominated sci-fi,&nbsp;
because, according to The Guardian, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.960
what publishers thought readers wanted.&nbsp;
That's probably a big reason why "Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.800
was rejected by more than 20 publishers&nbsp;
before it found a champion in Chilton.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.560
"Dune" proved that there was a market&nbsp;
not just for long-form science fiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:04.080
but high-end science fiction with literary merit.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.960
"Dune" is one of the first modern-day&nbsp;
sci-fi epics, and was thusly rewarded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.480
capturing the inaugural&nbsp;
Nebula Award for Best Novel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.000
Consumers responded in a big way to "Dune" and&nbsp;
its descendants. It's still a consistent seller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.200
and is one of the most-purchased sci-fi books&nbsp;
in history, while sequel "Children of Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.520
went down as the first novel in the genre&nbsp;
to become a bestseller in hardback form.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.520
Pre-production on the 1984 "Dune," including&nbsp;
the months it took writer-director David Lynch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.680
to adapt Frank Herbert's novel into&nbsp;
a screenplay, took a very long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.320
and filmmakers wanted to make sure they&nbsp;
cast the perfect performers to portray the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:46.240
potentially lucrative franchise's&nbsp;
already well-known characters.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.800
Glenn Close turned down the role of Jessica,&nbsp;
believing that the character was a "cliche" of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.600
a helpless woman. Aldo Ray was actually cast&nbsp;
as Gurney Halleck and reported to the set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.760
but was soon dismissed and replaced by&nbsp;
Patrick Stewart. Kenneth Branagh auditioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.520
for the lead role of Paul Atreides, but&nbsp;
lost out to newcomer Kyle MacLachlan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.160
Branagh's future partner, Helena Bonham Carter,&nbsp;
was also nearly a part of the "Dune" cast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.400
She'd landed the role of Princess Irulan, but&nbsp;
due to a scheduling conflict on "A Room with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.800
View," she had to quit the sci-fi epic, leaving&nbsp;
the door open for Virginia Madsen to take over.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.360
Although "Dune" is set among sophisticated&nbsp;
planets in the distant future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.800
the entertainment options of its residents have&nbsp;
a distinctively ancient and rustic sensibility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.360
Gurney Halleck is a major figure in the&nbsp;
life of Paul Atreides, a warrior, teacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:37.200
and ally who loved to perform old, minstrel-style&nbsp;
ballads to entertain guests and associates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.600
all while accompanying himself on a long,&nbsp;
nine-stringed instrument called the baliset.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.320
Something of a cross between a guitar and&nbsp;
a zither, it provided enough music to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.320
Halleck emphasize his proverbs,&nbsp;
scripture readings, and lyrics.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.880
In the 1984 film version of "Dune," Patrick&nbsp;
Stewart plays Halleck — and the baliset.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.760
It was mentioned by name in Frank Herbert's&nbsp;
original "Dune" novel, but filmmakers took a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:05.120
(although obscure) Earth instrument and painted&nbsp;
it gold. The baliset is actually a Chapman Stick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.280
a fretboard-based, elaborately strung,&nbsp;
strummable wooden instrument invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:14.080
by musician Emmett Chapman. In 1985,&nbsp;
Chapman released "Parallel Galaxy," an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:18.160
album of Chapman Stick compositions,&nbsp;
which includes "Backyard," heard in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.560
the Alan Smithee cut of the "Dune" movie — with&nbsp;
Stewart miming playing to Chapman's recording.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.160
At the time that "Dune" was&nbsp;
filmed in the early 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.320
Sean Young was one of the biggest new stars in&nbsp;
Hollywood thanks to prominent roles in the hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.040
comedy "Stripes" and the sci-fi classic "Blade&nbsp;
Runner." She signed on to play Chani Kynes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.480
the enchanting Fremen in "Dune" — but&nbsp;
she nearly missed out on the role.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:42.240
According to "The Making of Dune," Young's&nbsp;
agency set up a meeting in New York City with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.400
director David Lynch and producer Raffaella&nbsp;
De Laurentiis, but then forgot to tell Young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.760
about it. On the day it was scheduled,&nbsp;
she hopped a flight to California to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:53.920
meet about another film. Meanwhile, Lynch and&nbsp;
De Laurentiis missed their flight to the west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.600
coast because they'd been waiting around&nbsp;
for no-show Young. The actor, director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.040
and producer all wound up on the same plane, and&nbsp;
De Laurentiis asked a flight attendant if Young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.000
whom she'd never met or seen, was an actor. The&nbsp;
worker reportedly said, "She is. Her name is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.800
Sean Young." De Laurentiis confronted Young,&nbsp;
telling her that the agency said she refused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:18.160
the audition, which simply wasn't true. The&nbsp;
trio cleared the air over drinks. He recalled,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:21.280
"I sit with her and David and&nbsp;
we all start drinking champagne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.240
By the time we arrived in&nbsp;
L.A., we were roaring drunk."

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.760
Young got the part, of course.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.720
For the role of House Atreides&nbsp;
patriarch Duke Leto Atreides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.600
"Dune" writer-director David Lynch cast German&nbsp;
actor Jürgen Prochnow , the breakout star of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:40.560
the 1981 World War II submarine thriller&nbsp;
"Das Boot." According to "The Making of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.920
Dune," the last scene Prochnow had to film was a&nbsp;
drug-induced nightmare sequence in which he lays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:50.160
unconscious on a stretcher while the wicked&nbsp;
Baron Harkonnen, played by Kenneth McMillan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.880
crudely shoves his fingers into a facial&nbsp;
wound, expelling spooky green gas.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.280
To bring this concept to life, the "Dune"&nbsp;
special effects team created a fake cheek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.720
out of rubber and makeup, stuck it&nbsp;
to Prochnow's face, and attached a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:06.640
tube that ran behind the actor's ear&nbsp;
and onto the stretcher. Off-camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.960
a tech would pump green smoke into the device&nbsp;
that would plume out when McMillan prodded it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:15.840
The crew tested the effect on a dummy and Prochnow&nbsp;
before cameras rolled. When it came time to film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.160
McMillan did what he was supposed to do, sticking&nbsp;
his fingers into the wound, and the smoke came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.120
out. Something had gone wrong, however, because&nbsp;
Prochnow ran off the set, clutching his face.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.840
An investigation revealed that the device had&nbsp;
malfunctioned. It hadn't been properly sealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:34.960
and hot smoke from a test had built up inside&nbsp;
the fake cheek before McMillan tore it open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:38.560
resulting in near-molten goo&nbsp;
spilling onto Prochnow's face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.840
He suffered first and second&nbsp;
degree burns in the accident.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:46.080
David Lynch's "Dune" was obviously&nbsp;
supposed to be a franchise-starter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.720
a new blockbuster series to rival other sci-fi&nbsp;
brands like "Star Wars" and "Star Trek."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:55.120
Its success was so assured that before&nbsp;
the film's theatrical release in 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:59.280
star Kyle MacLachlan signed a contract to&nbsp;
appear in four more films in the "Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:04.000
cinematic universe. Virginia Madsen, who landed&nbsp;
the small role of Princess Irulan told "The Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:08.640
Pollak Chat Show" that she'd signed a deal to&nbsp;
reprise the role in two more films. She recalled,

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.040
"They thought they were gonna make 'Star Wars'&nbsp;
for grown-ups. And it didn't work out that way."

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:18.240
When "Dune" was greeted with critical shrugs and&nbsp;
less-than-huge numbers at the box office — a $30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:23.200
million total run, against a $40 million budget&nbsp;
— plans for more movies were canceled. "Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:27.440
screenwriter-director David Lynch was already&nbsp;
deep into the screenplay for the next movie in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:31.600
the series, based on Frank Herbert's "Dune&nbsp;
Messiah," which was subsequently abandoned.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:37.680
"Muad'dib had become the hand of God&nbsp;
fulfilling the fremen's prophecy."

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:42.880
Rather than seek out an orchestral composer to&nbsp;
create and conduct the score for the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:48.480
"Dune" film from 1984, filmmakers used rock band&nbsp;
Toto to source the movie's spooky, futuristic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:52.960
synth-based music. The group, consisting of&nbsp;
successful Los Angeles session musicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:58.080
was just coming off a career peak, winning&nbsp;
the Album of the Year Grammy Award in 1983&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.480
for "Toto IV," which included the&nbsp;
smash hits "Rosanna" and "Africa."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:06.080
The band was at a crossroads when it&nbsp;
came time to record the "Dune" music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:11.200
with singer Bobby Kimball having just departed and&nbsp;
the group deciding to score David Lynch's sci-fi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.160
adaptation instead of another gig it had been&nbsp;
offered: recording the soundtrack to "Footloose."

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:20.640
Nevertheless, the album flopped. Released&nbsp;
back-to-back with Toto's fifth studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:25.360
album "Isolation," the "Dune" soundtrack&nbsp;
tanked almost as hard as the movie did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:31.840
peaking at #168 on the Billboard album chart&nbsp;
and soon falling out of print for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:35.440
According to Vintage Guitar, members of&nbsp;
the group saw the film at its premiere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:40.080
and called it a "turkey" and privately&nbsp;
took to referring to the movie as "Doom."

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:46.080
Regardless of the film's lackluster performance,&nbsp;
the "Dune" franchise continued to gather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:50.000
more fans with each passing year.&nbsp;
The franchise itself also grew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:55.120
with a well-received miniseries airing on The&nbsp;
Sci-Fi Channel in 2003, and Herbert's son Brian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.120 --> 00:15:01.360
Herbert writing more bestselling "Dune" sequel and&nbsp;
prequel novels with Kevin J. Anderson. By 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:06.400
Hollywood once again saw "Dune" as a hot&nbsp;
property with a lot of commercial potential.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:10.720
Paramount announced plans for a new version&nbsp;
of the first novel, and hired Peter Berg, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.520
director of "Hancock" and "Battleship" to direct.&nbsp;
A year later, Berg dropped out and Pierre Morel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:20.080
of "Taken" fame replaced him, but the studio&nbsp;
called the whole thing off in March 2011.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:26.560
Once again, "Dune" refused to die: In 2016,&nbsp;
Legendary Entertainment restarted the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:31.680
again. Denis Villeneuve, the Oscar-nominated&nbsp;
director of "Arrival" and "Blade Runner 2049,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:36.320
signed on to direct a new adaptation of&nbsp;
Frank Herbert's original novel from 1965.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:39.280
With a budget reportedly around $200 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:45.920
it ranks among the most expensive undertakings in&nbsp;
Hollywood history — and that's just the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:49.680
as the project was planned from the&nbsp;
start to be split into two movies.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:54.720
Madsen claimed that filmmakers had attempted&nbsp;
to make "'Star Wars' for grown-ups." That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:59.120
the exact same approach that writer-director Denis&nbsp;
Villeneuve took when he set out to bring "Dune" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:04.160
the screen again, more than three decades after&nbsp;
that ill-fated attempt. Villeneuve told Fandom,

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:09.760
"The ambition is to do the 'Star Wars' movie I&nbsp;
never saw. In a way, it's 'Star Wars' for adults."

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:14.400
Ironically, 'Star Wars' began as a riff on&nbsp;
"Dune." George Lucas's first drafts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:18.720
script that would become 1977's "Star Wars:&nbsp;
A New Hope" contained fighting bloodlines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:23.440
and a princess who guarded not the plans for a&nbsp;
Death Star, but something called "aura spice."

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:27.360
The cinematographer on his new&nbsp;
"Dune" films? Grieg Fraser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:32.480
who had the same job on the "Star Wars"&nbsp;
spinoff "Rogue One," a film Villeneuve enjoyed.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:37.120
"Dune" is a passion project for the director, who&nbsp;
told press that in adapting the work correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:41.440
he dealt, quote, "with the pressure&nbsp;
of the dreams" he had as a teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:45.520
picturing the world of "Dune" in his head&nbsp;
as he read Frank Herbert's novel. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:50.960
when Warner Bros. wanted him to direct, he agreed&nbsp;
to do so if two conditions were met: "Dune" had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:56.240
be split into two films, and the crew would&nbsp;
film the Arrakis scenes in a real desert.

